  <!-- TODO: replace this placeholder with your real Irdeto description. -->
  <p>
    During my six-month internship at Irdeto, I worked as a visual designer within the marketing team. Irdeto is a company that specialises in digital security, protecting everything from video streaming and games to connected devices, so a big part of my work was finding ways to make a very technical field feel clear and approachable through visuals. My tasks included designing social media carousels for Irdeto’s LinkedIn page, mailing banners, icons and infographics for the website, as well as a desktop app icon for one of the company’s cybersecurity features. I also used Adobe Firefly for generative image work, extending and adapting visuals to fit different formats.

  </p>
  <p>
    Working inside an established brand system, built around Irdeto’s signature purple and green, taught me a very different kind of design discipline than my freelance and academic projects. Every piece had to feel consistent with the rest of the company’s communication, meet precise technical requirements such as pixel-perfect icons and exact export sizes, and go through feedback rounds with my supervisor. This experience showed me how much creativity there is in working within constraints, and how a strong visual system lets a whole team speak with one voice.

  </p>

